[RUNTIME] src/code_forge/verify.py:56-56 - The function _load_receipts now reads receipt files from disk and applies strict schema validation. Previously, malformed receipts (missing fields, wrong types, non-dict JSON) could either silently pass (using .get() defaults) or cause unhandled AttributeError/TypeError crashes. Now they raise CorruptedReceiptError, which run_verify catches and returns a clean failure. This changes the runtime behavior: any existing receipt that violates the schema will now cause verification to fail instead of potentially succeeding or crashing. Additionally, the OSError catch (line 75) now covers IsADirectoryError and other OS-level read failures, returning a corrupt-receipt error instead of propagating an unhandled exception. Without locking, concurrent writes to the receipts directory can result in partial reads, which are now consistently reported as corrupt rather than potentially causing race-condition-dependent crashes or silent failures.
[RUNTIME] src/code_forge/verify.py:215-215 - The run_verify function now catches CorruptedReceiptError from _load_receipts and returns a VerifyResult with passed=False and reason 'corrupt receipt: ...'. Previously, an exception would propagate to the caller; now callers must handle the returned failure instead of relying on exception handling. This change affects the runtime contract of the function. If callers expect exceptions to be raised (e.g., for logging or retry logic), they may break. However, the function's signature and return type remain the same, so the breakage is behavioral.
